### Failure attribution (why this run is red)

Source: the 'N tests failed:' recap (bun's authoritative, deduplicated list).
A failure is one `(fail)` line; the summary line ` N fail` is the total this must add up to.

| class | count | what it means |
|---|---:|---|
| real assertion failure | 0 | a test ran and its expectation failed - product or spec |
| environment / timeout | 0 | carries `this test timed out after Nms` - machine contention, not product |
| npm-artifact non-determinism | 1 | the byte-identity test's Buffer diff - known unrelated to the change under review |
| timeout cascade | 0 | `Unhandled error between tests` after a kill in the same file - the timeout's debris |
| unattributed error | 0 | a cascade in a file with no timeout marker; NOT excused, reported as unattributed |

Attribution is not waiver: every class above is still a failing test and this job is still red on
any of them. Nothing here converts a failure into a pass, and no gate below is relaxed.

**npm-artifact non-determinism (known flake)** - 1 (sample, bounded at 5):

```
package artifact emission > two generations of the same inputs produce byte-identical artifacts
```

Matched on test identity `package artifact emission > two generations of the same inputs produce byte-identical artifacts` **and** the binary-diff shape
(`expect(received).toEqual(expected)` over `"type": "Buffer"` at `tests/ts/package-artifacts.test.ts`).
Rendered diff lines: - Expected  - 7 + Received  + 7 

This is the pre-existing npm-artifact non-determinism the round-5 ruling names
(`docs/architecture/MANAGEMENT-RULING-005.md`, restoration condition 3). It is unrelated
to the change under review - which is exactly why it is labelled here instead of being
left to look like a product failure. It is **reported, not excused**: the suite step
above is still red on it.

Reconciliation: 1 failed test(s) attributed against the summary line's
`1 fail`; residual 0. A non-zero residual means the log's
failure text is not fully classified by these rules - say so, never round it away.

Limits of these rules, recorded rather than hidden: the timeout class needs the literal
`this test timed out after Nms` marker, so a test the runner kills without that marker
lands in `assertion`; the cascade rule needs the file-scope association bun does not
print itself; and a cascade in a file with no timeout is deliberately left unattributed.
Classifying a log is not running the suite - these counts describe this log only.
